INT. HOSPITAL ROOFTOP HELIPAD - NIGHT

Wind. Orange safety lights. The city spreads below like a spilled circuit board.

DR. MALACHI BOUSSARD-OJO (40s, scrubs under a parka) leans on the railing, staring at the lights. A thermos steams beside him.

Boots on metal stairs. The door opens.

A WOMAN IN A LONG GREY COAT (30s, hospital badge flipped backwards) steps out and stops five feet away.

MALACHI
You shouldn't be up here.

WOMAN
Neither should you.

MALACHI
I'm on break.

WOMAN
So am I.

She walks to the railing. Stands beside him. He pours coffee into the thermos cap, offers it. She takes it.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
Thanks, Dr. Boussard-Ojo.

MALACHI
You know my name.

WOMAN
It's on the schedule.

MALACHI
I don't know yours.

WOMAN
Not yet.

She sips. Looks at the city. He watches her. She hands the cap back, empty. He fills it again.

MALACHI
Third floor. Bed twelve. Did you know him?

WOMAN
Mr. Prokopiou?

MALACHI
Yes.

WOMAN
I did.

MALACHI
He died an hour ago.

WOMAN
I know. I was with him.

MALACHI
Not on the chart.

WOMAN
I'm not on any chart.

A siren climbs the hill and fades. Malachi stiffens. She looks at him steadily.

MALACHI
Who are you?

WOMAN
Delphine. Delphine Ashworth.

MALACHI
The inspector from the board?

DELPHINE
The same.

MALACHI
(low)
You were here the whole time.

DELPHINE
Since Tuesday.

His hand shakes. The thermos cap spills. He wipes his palm on his thigh, steps back, and she steps forward. He grips the railing. She grips his sleeve. Their eyes lock for a long time, wind snapping both coats against their legs.

DELPHINE (CONT'D)
Dr. Boussard-Ojo, the insulin dose you ordered was ten times the standard.

MALACHI
It was a clerical error.

DELPHINE
It was initialed in your handwriting.

MALACHI
(whispering)
I was tired.

DELPHINE
Everyone is tired.

He pulls free. Paces to the helipad's yellow circle. Stands in its center, head bowed.

MALACHI
(quietly)
He was in pain. For eleven days.

DELPHINE
And?

MALACHI
I thought I was doing him a kindness.

DELPHINE
You were doing yourself one.

He looks up. The wind drops. Far below, a patrol car pulls into the emergency bay, its lights spinning blue and red against the glass.

MALACHI
You called them.

DELPHINE
At the start of break.

MALACHI
Why wait?

DELPHINE
I wanted to hear you say it.

She picks up the thermos, screws the lid on, and sets it gently at his feet. He stares at it. Then slowly, he sits down in the yellow circle and lets the cold come.
